<header class="page-hero">
  <h1>Add ADS-B traffic to your Go&rsquo;s radar</h1>
  <p class="page-hero-subtitle">A Go and a Bravo make a Tango. Go transmits your position in ADS-L, the European standard for sharing position by radio. It shows ADS-L and FLARM traffic. FLARM is a position-sharing system widely used in gliding. Bravo detects Mode S and ADS-B transponders. Aircraft with ADS-B transmit their position by radio. Bravo sends that traffic to Go over Bluetooth, and all three types appear on the same radar.</p>
  <figure class="pair">
    <%= content_image_tag "pages/index/skyblip-go-techo.avif", alt: "skyBlip Go, a white LilyGO T-Echo with an antenna and traffic radar on its screen." %>
    <span class="pair-plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
    <%= content_image_tag "pages/skyblip-bravo/seeed-x1.avif", alt: "skyBlip Bravo, a black Seeed SenseCAP MeshTracker X1 about the size of a card.", class: "photo-x1" %>
  </figure>
  <p class="pair-caption">Shown to scale. X1 photo: Seeed Studio.</p>
</header>

<section id="traffic" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">Three types of traffic on one radar</h2>
  <p>On the same screen, see the glider fitted with FLARM, the microlight transmitting ADS-L and the aeroplane transmitting ADS-B. It could be a newer aeroplane or an older one upgraded with a glass cockpit connected to an ADS-B transmitter or with a new ADS-B transponder. Near an airfield you also see airliners and business jets transmitting ADS-B.</p>
  <%= render "shared/tango" %>
  <p>Go still transmits your position in ADS-L only. Connect your navigation app to Go to see the combined traffic. Bravo uses one Bluetooth connection on Go. That leaves two for your devices.</p>
</section>

<section id="alarm" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">The screen shows you where to look</h2>
  <p>When an alert triggers, part of Go&rsquo;s screen flashes in the direction of the traffic. If Go loses its link to Bravo, it shows <strong>BRAVO / NO LINK</strong> at the top right of the radar. Go still shows ADS-L and FLARM traffic. Bravo still receives ADS-B traffic and alerts on its own. To silence an alert, use the device reporting it.</p>
  <p>Tango is an uncertified aid to visual lookout. Keep looking outside, even when the screen is empty.</p>
</section>

<section id="versions" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">Tango or Tango+?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tango</strong>, with a Go and a Bravo: Go shows the alert. With sound on, Bravo sounds and vibrates.</li>
    <li><strong>Tango+</strong>, with a Go+ and a Bravo: Go+ shows the alert. With sound on, both devices sound and vibrate for the same alert.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>While the devices are linked, the ALARM setting on Go controls both. Press Bravo&rsquo;s button or hold Go&rsquo;s pad for one second to silence the alert on both devices.</p>
</section>

<section id="buy" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Put together a Tango with two devices</h2>
  <p>You buy the devices separately. If you already have a Go or Go+, get a MeshTracker X1 for Bravo. If you have both, Briefing helps you pair them on the ground.</p>
